<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Views"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:data="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Data.Converters;assembly=Avalonia.Base"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="960" d:DesignHeight="596"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.LightingTabView"
             x:DataType="vm:LightingTabViewModel">
  <!--
    The editor's Lighting tab, as design mockup 2f redraws it: THE MODE IS RENDERED ON THE BOARD,
    not named in a dropdown. Two columns — the board on the left, the mode rail on the right — and
    the board animates the selected effect at its real speed and direction while the rail is
    scrubbed.

    THE BOARD IS THE SCREEN'S SUBJECT, so it gets the canvas and everything else sits around it: the
    layer switch above it, its own header naming what it is showing, and the paint line under it.
    There is no `keyboardBoard` surround any more — the picture draws its own bordered section
    panels (Controls/KeyboardView.axaml), and an inset frame around them was the visible double
    frame issue #91 removed from the Layout canvas. This board now sits on a canvas of its own for
    exactly the same reason that one does.

    WHAT IS *NOT* HERE. No mode name, no parameter, no direction set, no zone and no speed bound is
    spelled in this file: LightingTabViewModel asks Core for every one of them
    (LightingModeParameters, LightingAvailability, LightingZoneCatalog, LayerLightingState). The
    frame timer that drives the animation is in the code-behind, and it does nothing but hand
    elapsed seconds to AdvancePreview — including the reduce-motion decision, which the view model
    re-reads on every call.
  -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">

    <!-- ===== The board ======================================================================= -->
    <Grid Grid.Column="0" RowDefinitions="Auto,*" Margin="0,0,16,0">

      <!-- The layer switch, relabelled "Lighting layer" (2f) — the two layers a led file describes
           are not the layout's five, and the switch says which of the two is being lit. The Fn
           segment is shown and dimmed below the LED 1.0.44 gate rather than hidden, because the
           layer exists and the reason is printed beside it. -->
      <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Spacing="8">
        <!-- Demo mode: everything is explorable, nothing is written (03 §3.5). -->
        <Border Classes="card" IsVisible="{Binding HasStatusMessage}">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" Classes="body muted" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        </Border>

        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="10">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{x:Static views:LightingTabView.LayerSwitchLabel}"
                     Classes="sectionLabel"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />

          <!-- The same segmented control the Keys tab's layer switch is, for the same reason: one
               of N, one tab stop. `SelectedItem` reads the panel one way — SelectLayer owns that
               property — and the selection change runs the command the pills used to. -->
          <ListBox Grid.Column="1"
                   Theme="{StaticResource SegmentedControl}"
                   ItemsSource="{Binding Layers}"
                   SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedLayer, Mode=OneWay}"
                   SelectionChanged="OnLayerSelectionChanged"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center">
            <!-- Scoped to this list, so it cannot outrank another one's container theme. -->
            <ListBox.Styles>
              <Style Selector="ListBoxItem" x:DataType="vm:LightingLayerViewModel">
                <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding IsEnabled}" />
              </Style>
            </ListBox.Styles>
            <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingLayerViewModel">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Caption}" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
          </ListBox>

          <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                     Text="{Binding LayerLockHint}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     IsVisible="{Binding !IsLayerCustomizationAvailable}" />
        </Grid>
      </StackPanel>

      <!-- The picture, centred as one block with the line that names it. `BoardHeader` is the whole
           point of this screen: "Wave · live preview" while it animates, "Wave · preview" while
           reduce-motion holds it on the effect's first frame. Sans — the mode's name is the app's
           own caption for it, not a token out of the file.

           The caps draw the LIT face (ShowsLighting) and NOT the state badges: the remap bar, the
           macro dot, the tap-and-hold corner and the advisory bar all report the *layout*, and this
           board cannot edit one. The two tabs render the same layer and the same cap view models;
           only what each picture draws differs.

           TOP-ALIGNED, NOT CENTRED, and that is a frame's finding rather than a preference. The
           board is the one thing on this screen the eye is fixed on while the rail is scrubbed, and
           it must not move under the pointer — so its position is fixed at the top of the column
           and anything under it grows downwards. (The defect that found this was the paint line
           appearing and disappearing with the mode, which slid a centred board ~70 px every time
           the rail's selection crossed between Wave and Freestyle. That row is now on screen in
           every mode, so the original cause is gone; the anchoring stays, because it is what makes
           the guarantee independent of what is drawn beneath.)

           A GRID, NOT A StackPanel (issue #123). A vertically-oriented StackPanel measures its
           children with double.PositiveInfinity down its own axis, so the picture's BoardScaleHost
           took its scale from the width alone and the block overflowed the slot — painted over its
           neighbours rather than cut, because nothing on the board chain clips. The board sits in
           the one star row and is therefore fitted to the height that is left after the header and
           the two rows beneath it. The former `Spacing="10"` is spelled into each row's own margin
           so the rhythm is unchanged: 10 under the header, 10+4 above the paint line, 10+8 above
           the zones. The block still starts at the top of the column — VerticalAlignment="Top" is
           what keeps the board still under the pointer while the rail is scrubbed. -->
      <Grid Grid.Row="1"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Margin="0,20,0,0"
            IsVisible="{Binding Board, Converter={x:Static data:ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
        <!-- The board's row carries a FLOOR, and the long form is what it takes to say so. The
             three Auto rows are measured before the star one and take everything they ask for, and
             on this screen they ask for a lot: at the 720x480 minimum the header, the paint line
             and the wrapped zone buttons want 231 of the column's 235px, which left the picture
             four pixels tall. Below HeightBoardMin the board stops giving way and the zone row -
             the bottom-most and least urgent thing on the screen - runs past the column instead,
             exactly as it does today at that window. -->
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
          <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="{StaticResource HeightBoardMin}" />
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0"
                   Text="{Binding BoardHeader}"
                   Classes="cardTitle"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   Margin="0,0,0,10" />

        <controls:KeyboardView Grid.Row="1"
                               DataContext="{Binding Board}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                               ShowsLighting="True"
                               ShowsStateBadges="False"
                               KeySelectedCommand="{Binding $parent[views:LightingTabView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).SelectKeyCommand}" />

        <!-- ===== The paint line ==============================================================
             2f's "Paint · 2 keys selected" with "Select all" and "Clear". It sits directly under
             the picture, exactly as the Layout tab's legend row does and for the same reason: it is
             a statement ABOUT the board, so it is the board's own width and its ends line up with
             the board's ends.

             IT IS ON SCREEN WHATEVER THE MODE IS, and 2f is the proof: the board it draws this row
             on is running WAVE, which ignores paint. The painted colours belong to the layer rather
             than to the effect over them — "the colors are still on file" — and the 40% paint layer
             exists precisely so they can be seen and edited under a mode that will not use them. A
             row that came and went with the mode would take the controls off the one screen the
             design chose to illustrate them on. Whether this board can paint at all is settled by
             the tab existing (LightingTabViewModel.IsSupported); the mode must not answer it twice.

             `Clear` turns the selected keys OFF rather than black, which is why the swatch rule
             beside it matters: an unlit key is hatched, never black. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="2"
              ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto"
              ColumnSpacing="8"
              Margin="0,14,0,0">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                     Text="{Binding Selection.Caption}"
                     Classes="body"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                     Text="{x:Static views:LightingTabView.HatchRule}"
                     Classes="meta muted"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Margin="8,0,0,0" />
          <Button Grid.Column="2"
                  Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:LightingPaintSelection.SelectAllCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding SelectAllKeysCommand}" />
          <Button Grid.Column="3"
                  Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:LightingPaintSelection.ClearCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding ClearKeyColorsCommand}" />
        </Grid>

        <!-- ===== The zones ===================================================================
             §4's named colour zones and the "Reset All" that erases every per-key colour: the bulk
             versions of the paint line above, so they sit with it rather than at the far end of the
             column — and on screen under the same rule, for the same reason.

             THEY ARE NOT FREE, and the note that used to say they were was written when the board
             was measured against infinite height. This row is an Auto row above a star one, so
             every line the zone buttons wrap onto is a line the picture does not get; at the
             720x480 minimum that is what takes the board down to its HeightBoardMin floor. A field
             that appears or vanishes with the mode still moves only itself — the block is
             top-anchored — but one that WRAPS now resizes the board with it. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="3"
              ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto"
              ColumnSpacing="8"
              Margin="0,18,0,0">
          <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Zones}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                <WrapPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
              </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate DataType="vm:LightingZoneViewModel">
                <Button Classes="zoneButton"
                        Content="{Binding Caption}"
                        Command="{Binding $parent[views:LightingTabView].((vm:LightingTabViewModel)DataContext).ApplyZoneCommand}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>

          <Button Grid.Column="1"
                  Classes="secondary"
                  Content="{x:Static vm:LightingTabViewModel.ResetAllCaption}"
                  Command="{Binding ResetAllCommand}"
                  VerticalAlignment="Top" />
        </Grid>
      </Grid>
    </Grid>

    <!-- ===== The mode rail ===================================================================
         Its own view, over this very DataContext: the rail is a 300px column with a header, a
         scrolled list, a disclosed picker and a pinned parameter footer, and folding all of that
         into this file would bury the board it is beside. -->
    <views:LightingModeRailView Grid.Column="1" />
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
